      "text": "<think>\nTo find the product of 829 and 522, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm. Here's how we do it step by step:\n\n1. **Multiply the ones place of the second number (2) by each digit in the first number (829), starting from the rightmost digit:**\n   - \\(2 \\times 9 = 18\\)\n   - \\(2 \\times 2 = 4\\)\n   - \\(2 \\times 8 = 16\\)\n\n   Write down the results, carrying over any tens digits:\n   ```\n     18\n    4\n   16\n   ```\n\n2. **Move to the tens place of the second number (2) and repeat the process:**\n   - \\(2 \\times 2 = 4\\)\n   - \\(2 \\times 9 = 18\\) (carry over 1)\n   - \\(2 \\times 8 = 16\\) (carry over 1)\n\n   Add the carried over digits:\n   ```\n     18\n    4\n   16\n   +1\n   +1\n   ```\n\n3. **Move to the hundreds place of the second number (5):**\n   - \\(5 \\times 9 = 45\\)\n   - \\(5 \\times 2 = 10\\) (carry over 1)\n   - \\(5 \\times 8 = 40\\) (carry over 1)\n\n   Add the carried over digits:\n   ```\n     45\n    10\n   40\n   +1\n   +1\n   ```\n\n4. **Combine all the results:**\n   ```\n     45\n    10\n   40\n   +1\n   +1\n   ```\n\n5. **Add the partial products together:**\n   ```\n     45\n    10\n   40\n   +1\n   +1\n   = 4510\n   ```\n\n6. **Finally, add the partial products together:**\n   ```\n     4510\n   +1618\n   = 61282\n   ```\n\nTherefore, the product of 829 and 522 is \\boxed{61282}. This is the final answer.\n</think>\n<answer>\n61282\n</answer>",
